c6=A.bh(c3,"8-6",A.a([c9,d1,d2,A.h(f7,0,A.a([0,2,3],p),"Against a player who is close, safety comes first, so read their discards and visible copies rather than protecting shape.",c6,f7,"Turn 12. You are two tiles from ready and one opponent holds three melds. Which actions are correct?",f7,B.D),A.h(f7,0,f7,"These four ideas are the whole tactical toolkit: read the table, judge safety, know when to fold, and count your outs.",B.p,B.UH,"Match each tactic to what it means.",f7,B.G),A.h(f7,0,f7,"A tile they already discarded is safe this rotation, three visible copies leave only one live copy, and an unseen tile leaves all four.",B.p,f7,"An opponent is one tile from winning. Arrange these discards from safest to most dangerous: {{red}}, which they threw earlier this rotation; {{p9}}, of which three copies are visible; {{s2}}, which nobody has played.",B.Ui,B.ad),A.h(A.a(["1","one","1 copy","one copy"],k7),0,f7,"Four copies exist in total, so with three visible exactly one copy is still live and it may be in any hand or the wall.",B.p,f7,"Three copies of {{s8}} are visible on the table. How many copies can an opponent still be holding?",f7,B.A),A.h(f7,0,f7,"Discard reading and copy counting drive both your own wait choices and your safety judgements, so one habit serves both sides.",A.a(["Watching every discard and counting visible copies","Calling the first meld offered every hand","Never discarding honour tiles","Always keeping one pair in hand"],k7),f7,"Which single habit improves both your attack and your defence?",f7,B.i)],o),j2,"Basic tactics mastery","Stage 8 Review")
d2=A.a([A.L(f6,"Advanced players use deception! You can mislead opponents about what you're building through strategic discards.",f7,f7),A.L(f6,"Example: You're building a Bamboo flush. Discard a Bamboo tile early (like {{s2}}) to make opponents think you DON'T want Bamboo!",f7,f7),A.L(f6,"Early discards carry the strongest signal. If you throw {{s2}} on turn 1, opponents assume Bamboo is safe to discard to you. Perfect trap!",f7,f7),A.L(f6,'Counter-bluffing: If an opponent\'s discards seem "too perfect," they might be bluffing! Experienced players question obvious patterns.',f7,f7)],k5)
